1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
 So this is a challenge for you, I mean a challenge that is not in the book but that is challenging your critical thinking.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:21,000
 If you google replacement models or you go to SignDirect and you look for replacement models you find a different type of models.

3
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
 Well, I don't know, it's up to you to decide whether they are different or similar.

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,000
 I give here two examples, so we have here a book, maintenance, replacement and reliability.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:39,000
 And here we have a survey of replacement models and there are thousands of maintenance replacement models around.

6
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
 So let's have a look what type of questions do these models answer.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
 Well, for instance, I have an asset for which maintenance costs keep increasing every year.

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
 Or I have an asset for which the probability of failure keeps increasing with time.

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
 What is the best time to replace this asset with a new one?

10
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:07,000
 Or if I use a replacement policy, should I base myself on asset age or maybe fixed period?

11
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 And if I have a set of identical devices, should I then replace them one by one or should I invest more money and replace them all at once?

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
 So these are the type of questions.

13
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 I have a very small example of such a model for you, a very simple one.

14
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:35,000
 So we are talking about small welding robots costing 7000 euros, maximum use for life is 5 years,

15
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 maintenance costs are CK, which are expressed as 1000K and K being the equipment age.

16
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
 We have some historical data and supplier information on probabilities of unrepairable failures that are given there.

17
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:58,000
 So first year is 10%, second year is 10%, third year is 20% and so on.

18
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
 And yearly we can decide whether to replace the equipment by a new one or not.

19
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:07,000
 And then they ask what is the optimum replacement timing.

20
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:22,000
 To the right you see the expected life of the robot using the failure probabilities and the expected costs that we have that are associated with that life.

21
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:36,000
 And then there we see that also the costs that we compute, expected costs divided by expected life.

22
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:44,000
 And then we see that here after 4 years seems to be the best time to replace the robot.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
 This is a repair limit problem, that is what it is called in literature.

24
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 It is one of the types of replacement models.

25
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
 So basically what we do is we replace the robot when maintenance costs become too high.

26
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,000
 The theory that is responsible for these type of formulas is called renewal theory.

27
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:15,000
 It is part of operations research models specifically developed for this type of problems.

28
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
 There are two hypotheses.

29
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
 One hypothesis is as good as new.

30
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 Meaning that each time of course if you buy a new robot you have a new robot.

31
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:30,000
 That is fine.

32
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:39,000
 But it is also saying that if you do corrective maintenance the robot is as good as new.

33
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
 Which is not always maybe realistic.

34
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,000
 But we are not considering that here.

35
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,000
 But if you do preventive maintenance also you make the machine as good as new.

36
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,000
 And then we can use this formula which we did there.

37
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:58,000
 So my question to you is, it is called replacement model.

38
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,000
 It is about replacing assets, finding the right time to replace an asset.

39
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,000
 What differences, what similarities do you see?

40
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 So it just requires some critical thinking of you.

41
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:18,000
 We will come back to that with the exercises.

